module Core

import Native

{-
   The block bodies of `HexKernel.yana`, natively, at the **natural width** (J238): `encodeHexBlocks` -
   a register of bytes read and two of units written a turn - and `decodeHexBlocks`, two registers of
   units read and one of bytes written. `vectorBytes` is sixteen at `sse4`, thirty-two at `avx2` and
   sixty-four at `avx512`, and each level gets its own width from this one text. Since J235 they are
   also `hexOf`'s and `readHexBytes`' (`Hex.native.yana` lends each the reserved run of its string or
   array as a byte window), which until then had loops of their own here.

   **The digit is a lookup** - the classic `pshufb` of a sixteen-entry table, which is `lookup` since
   Analysis-Avx512.md §3.3. The table stays sixteen bytes and is read at the natural width's indices:
   one `pshufb` at `sse4`, one `vpshufb` of the table in both halves at `avx2`, one `vpermi2b` of it
   repeated at `avx512` - the repetition built once, beside the table, in front of the loop (J238: the
   x64 backend's `repeatTable`, `compiler/codegen/x64/transform_move.cpp`; until then it stood at each
   lookup, twice a turn, once `upper` made the table a value chosen at run time). A table of the
   natural width read at as many indices would be the same at `sse4` and `avx512` and a
   five-instruction crossing read at `avx2`, which cannot know that its two halves are one table. The
   lookup takes each index modulo sixteen, which is what lets the low nibble be the byte itself, with
   no `and` in front of it.

   **The interleave is the width's own**: two `punpck`s at `sse4`, both in-half unpacks and two
   `vperm2f128`s at `avx2` (J236), a `vpermi2b` each at `avx512`. Until J238 the baseline was pinned at
   sixteen lanes because `avx2` refused the interleave, and `.native.avx512.yana` was this loop at
   sixty-four; one body is both now.

   **The ragged end is one more block**, read past the end on §5.2's padding - the input is a window,
   which carries it - and its units written up to their count with `storeUpTo`, so the body takes every
   byte that fits and writes nothing past them: a write past the window would be the destruction of
   whatever follows it. Its `r` bytes are `2r` units: the first register's worth in the low interleave
   and the rest, if any, in the high one, whose count is then at or below zero and writes nothing -
   the low store's count may pass its lanes, which `storeUpTo` takes as the whole register.

   `@inline(always)`, for `decodeBase64Blocks`' reason (J234 §5): at `@inline` the inliner may keep the
   body out of `hexOf`, which then passes the window and the answer through memory.

   This is the native *baseline*, not a replacement - `HexKernel.yana` says why.
-}
@inline(always) fn encodeHexBlocks(upper: Bool, input: '[U8], &space: '[U8]) -> CodecProgress:
    let room = length(space) `shr` 1
    let n = if length(input) < room then length(input) else room
    if n <= 0 then return CodecProgress {read: 0, written: 0}

    let source = input.items
    let target = space.items
    let digits = hexDigitVector(upper)
    let width = vectorBytes

    let &i = 0 :: Size
    while i + width <= n:
        let block = vectorAt(source + i) :: Vec(U8)
        let highText = lookup(digits, block `shr` (splat(4) :: Vec(U8)))
        let lowText = lookup(digits, block)
        setVectorAt(target + i + i, interleaveLow(highText, lowText))
        setVectorAt(target + i + i + width, interleaveHigh(highText, lowText))
        i = i + width

    if i < n:
        let block = vectorPast(source + i) :: Vec(U8)
        let highText = lookup(digits, block `shr` (splat(4) :: Vec(U8)))
        let lowText = lookup(digits, block)
        let units = truncate((n - i) + (n - i)) :: Int
        let lanes = truncate(width) :: Int

        -- The `and` changes no value - `units` is at most `2 * width - 2` - but it is a range the
        -- backend reads: with it the masked store's count needs no clamp, which at `avx512` is a sign
        -- test, a compare and two `cmov`s in front of the `bzhi`, and a 16-byte `hexOf` 2-3% (J240).
        storeUpTo(target + i + i, units `and` (lanes + lanes - 1), interleaveLow(highText, lowText))
        storeUpTo(target + i + i + width, units - lanes, interleaveHigh(highText, lowText))

    return CodecProgress {read: n, written: n + n}

{-
   Each byte's high nibble for the decoder, by a word shift and the mask the low nibble's lookup uses
   too. A byte-lane `shr` is that shift and a mask of its own, which the backend hoists to the
   function's entry in a callee-saved register - at `avx512` a sixty-four-byte save and restore around
   every call of the function it is inlined into, which cost `readHexBytes` 7% at sixteen bytes (J235
   §5). The encoder keeps the byte-lane `shr` and the lookup's own modulus: written this way there,
   a sixteen-byte `hexOf` was 8% slower. The block comes in as its words, so the one text serves the
   sixteen-lane loop and the natural width.
-}
@inline(always) fn (k: Int, n: Int) hexHighNibbles(words: Vec(U16, k), low: Vec(U8, n)) -> Vec(U8, n) =
    (bitcast(words `shr` (splat(4 :: U16) :: Vec(U16, k))) :: Vec(U8, n)) `and` low

-- `hexDigitUnits`' sixteen digits of the case asked for, as a vector for `lookup`: one load. Until
-- J235 it was computed from `iota`, which folded while there was one case and was a dozen instructions
-- in front of every call once `upper` was a parameter.
@inline fn hexDigitVector(upper: Bool) -> Vec(U8, 16) =
    if upper then loadVectorAt(hexDigitUnits, 16) :: Vec(U8, 16) else loadVectorAt(hexDigitUnits, 0) :: Vec(U8, 16)

{-
   `decodeHexBlocks`, classified by **nibble tables** - `decodeBase64Blocks16`'s route
   (Muła's) at this notation's size, where `readHexBytes`' block loop before J235 subtracted each of the
   three runs a digit can fall in and tested each: three subtractions, three comparisons, two `or`s and
   two selects with their adds, against two lookups, an `and` and a comparison here.

   `hi` gives each high nibble a class bit - 1 the digit row (3), 2 the two letter rows (4 and 6), 4
   every other row - and `lo` sets the bits of the classes that low nibble is *not* a digit in, so a unit
   is a digit exactly when `lo[unit & 15] & hi[unit >> 4]` is zero. The value is the unit plus `roll` at
   its high nibble, as a byte that wraps: `-48` for the digits, `-55` and `-87` for the two cases of
   letters. A unit at or above 128 is in a row of class 4, which every column of `lo` refuses.

   **A pair is a little-endian word and the first unit is its low byte**, so `(pair << 4) | (pair >> 8)`
   has the byte in its low half - the first digit moved up a nibble, the second down a byte - and what
   the shift left moves into the high half is not read: the bytes are every other lane, which is one
   `pshufb`, and the eight of them are one quadword store. So a block writes exactly its bytes and the
   loop wants eight bytes of room.
-}
let hexNibbles = [
    -- lo: the classes each low nibble is not a digit in
    6, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 6, 6, 6, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7,
    -- hi: each high nibble's class
    4, 4, 4, 1, 2, 4, 2, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4,
    -- roll: what a unit of that high nibble adds to be its value
    0, 0, 0, 208, 201, 0, 169, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0
] :: [U8 *48]

-- The sixteen-lane loop from `from` and `at` on: the tail of the natural-width one, for what is left
-- of the run once fewer than two registers of units are.
@inline fn decodeHexBlocks16(input: '[U8], &space: '[U8], from: Size, at: Size) -> CodecProgress:
    let count = length(input)
    let room = length(space)
    if from + 16 > count || at + 8 > room then return CodecProgress {read: from, written: at}

    let source = input.items
    let target = space.items

    let lo = loadVectorAt(hexNibbles, 0) :: Vec(U8, 16)
    let hi = loadVectorAt(hexNibbles, 16) :: Vec(U8, 16)
    let roll = loadVectorAt(hexNibbles, 32) :: Vec(U8, 16)
    let low = splat(15 :: U8) :: Vec(U8, 16)
    let empty = zero() :: Vec(U8, 16)

    let &i = from
    let &o = at

    while i + 16 <= count && o + 8 <= room:
        let block = vectorAt(source + i) :: Vec(U8, 16)
        let high = hexHighNibbles(bitcast(block) :: Vec(U16, 8), low)
        if !all((lookup(lo, block `and` low) `and` lookup(hi, high)) .== empty) then break

        let pairs = bitcast(block + lookup(roll, high)) :: Vec(U16, 8)
        let joined = (pairs `shl` (splat(4 :: U16) :: Vec(U16, 8))) `or` (pairs `shr` (splat(8 :: U16) :: Vec(U16, 8)))
        let packed = shuffle(bitcast(joined) :: Vec(U8, 16), [0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15])
        store(bitcast(target + o) :: %U64, lane(bitcast(packed) :: Vec(U64, 2), 0))

        i = i + 16
        o = o + 8

    return CodecProgress {read: i, written: o}

-- One register of units classified and turned into its pairs' words, each byte in its word's low half;
-- `bad` is nonzero in the lane of each unit that is not a digit.
@inline(always) fn hexJoinedPairs(block: Vec(U8), lo: Vec(U8, 16), hi: Vec(U8, 16), roll: Vec(U8, 16),
                                  low: Vec(U8)) -> {joined: Vec(U16), bad: Vec(U8)}:
    let high = hexHighNibbles(bitcast(block) :: Vec(U16), low)
    let bad = lookup(lo, block `and` low) `and` lookup(hi, high)
    let pairs = bitcast(block + lookup(roll, high)) :: Vec(U16)
    return {joined: (pairs `shl` (splat(4 :: U16) :: Vec(U16))) `or` (pairs `shr` (splat(8 :: U16) :: Vec(U16))), bad: bad}

{-
   `decodeHexBlocks` at the natural width: **two registers of units a turn and one of bytes**, which is
   what makes the store a whole register at every width - `packLanes` of the two registers' words is
   `packuswb` over each (after the `and` its truncation is), plus the quadword exchange that puts the
   halves in order at `avx2` and `avx512`. One register a turn would leave half a register of bytes,
   which is a `movq` at sixteen lanes and a masked or extracted store at the others. The two blocks'
   classifications are `or`ed and tested once.

   What is left after the last pair of registers - fewer than `2 * vectorBytes` units - goes to the
   sixteen-lane loop above, which takes sixteen units at a time down to the last fifteen.
-}
@replaces @inline(always)
fn decodeHexBlocks(input: '[U8], &space: '[U8]) -> CodecProgress:
    let count = length(input)
    let room = length(space)
    let width = vectorBytes
    let source = input.items
    let target = space.items

    let &i = 0 :: Size
    let &o = 0 :: Size

    if count >= width + width && room >= width:
        let lo = loadVectorAt(hexNibbles, 0) :: Vec(U8, 16)
        let hi = loadVectorAt(hexNibbles, 16) :: Vec(U8, 16)
        let roll = loadVectorAt(hexNibbles, 32) :: Vec(U8, 16)
        let low = splat(15 :: U8) :: Vec(U8)
        let empty = zero() :: Vec(U8)

        while i + width + width <= count && o + width <= room:
            let first = hexJoinedPairs(vectorAt(source + i) :: Vec(U8), lo, hi, roll, low)
            let second = hexJoinedPairs(vectorAt(source + i + width) :: Vec(U8), lo, hi, roll, low)
            if !all((first.bad `or` second.bad) .== empty) then break

            setVectorAt(target + o, packLanes(first.joined, second.joined) :: Vec(U8))

            i = i + width + width
            o = o + width

    return decodeHexBlocks16(input, space, i, o)
